  "rawCandidate": "```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"selectedContextPaths\": [\n    \"checks/test_parser.py\",\n    \"README.md\",\n    \"src/parser.py\"\n  ],\n  \"plan\": {\n    \"mutationTargets\": [],\n    \"requestedCheckIds\": []\n  },\n  \"answer\": {\n    \"kind\": \"read_only\",\n    \"files\": [],\n    \"citations\": [\n      \"checks/test_parser.py\",\n      \"README.md\",\n      \"src/parser.py\"\n    ],\n    \"findingIds\": [],\n    \"summary\": \"The explicit false case fails because parse_enabled does not parse the string's literal content; it returns bool(value), which coerces via Python truthiness. Any non-empty string is truthy, so bool(\\\"false\\\") evaluates to True. Thus parse_enabled(\\\"false\\\") returns True, and the fixture assertion assert parse_enabled(\\\"false\\\") is False fails because True is False is False. The true case passes only by coincidence: bool(\\\"true\\\") is also True, so assert parse_enabled(\\\"true\\\") is True holds. The README contract states the parser 'should recognize explicit true and false values,' a behavior the current bool()-wrapping implementation does not satisfy for the false case.\"\n  }\n}\n```",
